# UNVERIFIED. Not built, not run on a device or desktop machine. Written at
# the explicit request of a user who chose "attempt it anyway, flagged" after
# being told the honest cost: this is new C++ reasoning, not a flag flip, and
# the usual verification this project relies on for that (patch -p1 --fuzz=0
# against pristine Chromium, a mechanical sed-chain simulation) proves the
# patch applies cleanly, not that the resulting binary behaves correctly. Do
# not carry this into a release without an actual build and a real low-memory
# run on Linux hardware.
#
# What this is: aerium-runtime-efficiency.patch enables
# kInfiniteTabsFreezingOnMemoryPressure, which extends tab freezing to fire
# under memory pressure - but only on Windows. The reason, read directly out
# of FreezingPolicy::CheckMemoryPressureForFreezing() in pristine Chromium
# rather than assumed: the function is written entirely inside
# `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)`, with no Linux branch at all, and its own comment
# in this repo's aerium-runtime-efficiency.patch already says as much - "the
# branch that reads it is inside #if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN) in freezing_policy.cc".
#
# What looked at first like a gap needing a whole new
# SystemMemoryPressureEvaluator subclass (components/memory_pressure/, with
# platform files for Windows/Mac/Fuchsia and none for Linux, confirmed by
# reading components/memory_pressure/BUILD.gn directly) turns out not to be
# the real mechanism this feature uses at all. CheckMemoryPressureForFreezing
# does not go through that system - it calls base::GetSystemMemoryInfo()
# directly, a much simpler, older API that base/process/process_metrics.h
# documents as already cross-platform: "On Linux/Android/Chrome OS,
# system-wide memory consumption data is parsed from /proc/meminfo and
# /proc/vmstat. On Windows/Mac, it is obtained using system API calls." Linux
# support already exists and is already used elsewhere in Chromium (the Task
# Manager's memory column, among others) - this function just never asked it
# the question because the guard around it never let Linux in the door.
#
# The one platform-specific detail inside the guarded block is
# `info.avail_phys`, a field that base/process/process_metrics.h declares
# only `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)` - Linux's available-memory estimate lives in a
# same-shaped but differently-named field (`available`, sourced from the
# kernel's own MemAvailable, present since Linux 3.14) guarded
# `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX) || ...`. Rather than add a second platform branch
# to read the right field name, this uses SystemMemoryInfo's own
# GetAvailablePhysicalMemory() - declared right next to both fields as "a
# cross-platform estimation of available physical memory" - which is what it
# exists for. It is a no-op change on Windows: GlobalMemoryStatusEx already
# populates avail_phys there, and GetAvailablePhysicalMemory() reads that
# same field back on that platform.
#
# The percent threshold (kInfiniteTabsFreezingOnMemoryPressurePercent,
# default 15) and the poll interval are both existing feature params already
# enabled by aerium-runtime-efficiency.patch - nothing about the threshold or
# cadence changes here, only which platforms the check runs on.
#
# Windows-only in this repo pair deliberately: the `|| BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)`
# widening is inert on a Windows build (IS_WIN already made the guard true),
# so carrying an identical no-op patch into the Windows repo would just be
# diff for its own sake. This is the one place this project's "the two patch
# sets are identical" rule is deliberately not followed, and the reason is
# written here rather than left for PARITY.md to explain on its own.
#
# What is and is not verified:
#   * base::GetSystemMemoryInfo() having a real, already-shipping Linux
#     implementation, and SystemMemoryInfo::GetAvailablePhysicalMemory()
#     existing as its documented cross-platform accessor - both read directly
#     out of base/process/process_metrics.h at the pinned Chromium version,
#     not assumed.
#   * The two `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)` guards and the `avail_phys` read are
#     exactly as pristine Chromium has them at this version - confirmed by
#     fetching freezing_policy.cc itself, not recalled from memory.
#   * The patch applies cleanly with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` against a pristine
#     copy of freezing_policy.cc at the pinned tag.
#   * NOT verified: that FreezingPolicy's surrounding code has no other
#     Windows-only assumption nearby that this reading missed. The function
#     itself was read in full; the rest of the file was not.
#   * NOT verified at all: that this compiles, or that a real Linux machine
#     under genuine memory pressure freezes tabs the way it is meant to. No
#     build was run, on this or any patch in this file.
--- a/components/performance_manager/freezing/freezing_policy.cc
+++ b/components/performance_manager/freezing/freezing_policy.cc
@@ -260,15 +260,15 @@
     resource_usage_query_observation_.Observe(&resource_usage_query_.value());
